Problem Statement: Set the i-th Bit
Given a number n and an integer i, write a program to set the i-th bit from the right (0-indexed) of n to 1. If the i-th bit is already 1, the number should remain unchanged.
You must provide two approaches:
Brute Force Approach: Avoid bitwise operators entirely by calculating powers of 2 and using basic arithmetic to check and update the bit.
Bit Manipulation Approach: Use the bitwise left shift (<<) and bitwise OR (|) operators for an optimal O(1) solution.

Brute Force: (Using Arithemetic)
The i-th bit represents the value 2^i.
We can check if this bit is a 1 by dividing our number by 2^i and checking if the result is odd or even. 
If it evaluates to even (0) the bit is currently 0 and we can set it to 1 by simplying adding 2^i to our number.

int setBitBruteForce(int n, int i) {
    //Calculate 2^i using a simple loop to avoid bitwise shifts 
    int powerOfTwo = 1;
    for (int k = 0; k < i; ++k) {
        powerOfTwo *= 2; 
    }

    //Check if the i-th bit is 0 using division and modulo 
    //If (n / 2^i) % 2 is 0, the bit at position i is 0
    if ((n / powerOfTwo) % 2 == 0) {
        n += powerOfTwo; //Add 2^i to set the bit to 1
    }

    return n;
}

int main() {
    int n = 9;  // Binary: 1001
    int i = 1;  // We want to set the bit at index 1
    
    std::cout << "Original number: " << n << "\n";
    int result = setBitBruteForce(n, i);
    std::cout << "After setting " << i << "-th bit (Brute Force): " << result << "\n"; 
    // Expected Output: 11 (Binary: 1011)
    
    return 0;
}

TC: O(i)
coz we run a loop i times to calculate 2^i.
SC: O(1)

Approach 2: Using the Left Shift and OR (<< and ||)
We create a mask by taking a number 1 and shifting it left by i positions (1 << i).
This creates a binary number where only the i-th bit is 1 and all others are 0.

We apply the bitwise OR (|) operator between the original number n and this mask 
the i-th bit becomes 1 (coz anything ORed with 1 is 1) and all other bits remain completely unchanged (coz anything ORed with 0 stays its original value).

int setBitLeftShift(int n, int i) {
    //Create the mask (1 shifted by i) and apply bitwise OR 
    return n | (1 << i);
}

int main() {
    int n = 9;  // Binary: 1001
    int i = 2;  // We want to set the bit at index 2
    
    std::cout << "Original number: " << n << "\n";
    int result = setBitLeftShift(n, i);
    std::cout << "After setting " << i << "-th bit (Left Shift): " << result << "\n";
    // Expected Output: 13 (Binary: 1101)
    
    return 0;
}

TC: O(1) as creating the mask and applying the bitwise OR takes a single constant-time CPU instance.

SC: O(1)
